WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:03.129
INTRO:
Recently, I was watching an episode of millennial

00:00:03.129 --> 00:00:17.410
money when I heard this.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:22.210
While the article was an April Fool’s joke,
it did get me wondering: what happened to

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:23.499
the Cybertruck?

00:00:23.499 --> 00:00:28.699
When Elon Musk announced the vehicle at the
end of 2019, we saw some of the most polarizing

00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:30.009
reactions.

00:00:30.009 --> 00:00:34.930
Many were quick to laugh at it calling it
a polygon on wheels, while others felt that

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:39.340
this is exactly what Tesla needed in order
to stick out inside of the saturated pick

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:40.430
up truck market.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:44.940
Following the announcement, hundreds of thousands
of people put in pre orders which eventually

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.580
turned into millions.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:50.780
While this level of interest is great, it
doesn’t exactly mean much given that the

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:57.370
deposit amount is only $100 and the lowest
end cyber truck starts at just under $40,000.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:01.790
With all of that being said though, the cybertruck
was supposed to hit the road months ago at

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:03.900
the end of 2021.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:09.729
This was originally postponed to 2022, but
at the beginning of the year, this was postponed

00:01:09.729 --> 00:01:11.469
indefinitely.

00:01:11.469 --> 00:01:15.920
Tesla has gone ahead and removed all delivery
estimates from their website, and you can’t

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.590
even configure a cybertruck anymore.

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:21.420
You have to wait until production nears to
do that.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.609
On top of this, in the latest quarterly earnings
call, when Elon was questioned about whether

00:01:25.609 --> 00:01:31.049
Tesla would start roadster or cybertruck production
this year, he responded with quote that “wouldn’t

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:32.200
make any sense”.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.290
Clearly, it doesn’t look like the Cybertruck
will be coming out anytime soon.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:40.810
So, here’s when the Cybertruck may actually
hit the roads and the main factors that are

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:44.399
holding back Cybertruck production.

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:49.280
PRICE:
One of the biggest factors holding back Cybertruck

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.619
production is rampant inflation.

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:54.619
I’m sure you guys have seen the numbers.

00:01:54.619 --> 00:02:00.959
Month after month, we’re seeing CPI readings
come in at 5, 6, 7%, and one of the biggest

00:02:00.959 --> 00:02:03.929
culprits boosting these readings is car prices.

00:02:03.929 --> 00:02:09.259
Originally, this price hike was mainly manifesting
within the used car market as supply was extremely

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:13.700
low, but this has slowly carried over to the
new market as well.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.290
After all, basically every raw material you
can think of, semiconductors, and manual labor

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:20.010
have all risen substantially.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:24.819
So, it’s not surprising that it costs a
lot more to produce the same vehicle today

00:02:24.819 --> 00:02:26.750
than it did a few years ago.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:31.629
And Tesla is just as susceptible to these
price hikes as any other automotive company.

00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:36.240
The model 3, for example, reached a price
of $35,000 at the start of 2019.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.670
Now, it should be noted that you had to jump
through a lot of hoops and give up a lot of

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.959
features to get a model 3 for this price.

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:44.720
But, it was possible.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:51.409
Today, however, the lowest end model 3 costs
$47,000 or $12,000 more.

00:02:51.409 --> 00:02:56.220
Not to mention, the self driving package price
has increased to $12,000 itself.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:00.549
Meanwhile, hundreds of thousands of people
have locked in a cybertruck for $40,000 and

00:03:00.549 --> 00:03:03.000
self driving for $8,000.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.640
This means that these guys literally got a
cybertruck with self driving for the same

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.959
price as a base level model 3.

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:14.360
Realistically, Tesla would charge a minimum
of $70,000 for such a vehicle today meaning

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:18.690
that they’re in the hole by $22,000 on each
base model cybertruck.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:23.829
Now, while Elon promised that FSD prices would
be honored if locked in, Tesla doesn’t have

00:03:23.829 --> 00:03:29.030
to honor the purchase price of the Cybertruck
itself, but at the same time, they kind of

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:30.030
do.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:34.400
While Tesla won’t face any hurdles legally
when it comes to increasing the price of Cybertrucks,

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.920
they would likely face a PR nightmare if they
increased prices.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.410
Just take a look at what happened to Rivian.

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:44.420
Similar to the Cybertruck, Rivian revealed
their R1T pick up truck well before the pandemic,

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:46.750
and they priced these models accordingly.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:52.100
But, with rampant inflation, Rivian decided
to increase prices by as much as $20,000 even

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.020
for preorder customers.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:58.999
Over the next few days, Rivian received so
much backlash and so many people canceled

00:03:58.999 --> 00:04:03.930
that Rivian reversed this decision and announced
that they would honor pre order prices.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:08.109
Considering this, it would be extremely difficult
for Tesla to get away with raising pre order

00:04:08.109 --> 00:04:11.609
prices, and the longer the wait to start production
the worse.

00:04:11.609 --> 00:04:15.780
The last thing you wanna do is to tell someone
who’s been waiting 6 years for the cybertruck

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:19.010
that they have to now pay $30,000 more to
get it.

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:24.020
So, the best option Tesla has is to simply
take a loss on these vehicles and move on.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:27.780
But, when it’s basically guaranteed to be
a loss, there’s no urgency to push out the

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:28.780
Cybertruck.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:32.650
It makes a lot more sense to scale model 3
and y production first and get to an even

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:35.389
better financial position before launching
the cybertruck.

00:04:35.389 --> 00:04:40.280
And with the Germany and Austin factories
just now starting production, these factories

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:45.030
will likely have their plates full with just
scaling production till at least 2024.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:51.220
So, in my opinion, 2024 is likely the earliest
that Tesla will focus on Cybertruck production.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:57.270
LACK OF INTEREST:
Aside from increasing prices, something else

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:01.710
that’s pushing back cybertruck production
is a lack of interest from average consumers.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:06.220
Now, before Tesla fanboys go to the comment
section and vigorously type about how many

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:08.870
pre-orders Tesla has and how Tesla is going
to crush the competition, let me explain.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:12.470
While people who preordered the Cybertruck
and Tesla enthusiasts are still super pumped

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:16.389
up about cybertruck, the average person never
really cared.

00:05:16.389 --> 00:05:20.610
At the end of the day, given how the cybertruck
looks, it’s simply not even a consideration

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:23.590
for a significant proportion of the population.

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:27.180
And if you don’t believe me, just take a
look at Google Trends.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:31.889
If we compare Model 3 and cybertruck interest,
we’ll see that not only was initial excitement

00:05:31.889 --> 00:05:36.919
for the model 3 slightly higher than the cybertruck,
but model 3 interest has stabilized at a significantly

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:37.919
higher level.

00:05:37.919 --> 00:05:42.199
On average, Model 3 interest has been 3-4
times higher than the Cybertruck over the

00:05:42.199 --> 00:05:43.550
past 12 months.

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:48.699
Now you might be saying that, well, so many
people have already received the model 3 and

00:05:48.699 --> 00:05:53.550
by this point, most people in developed countries
see model 3s quite regularly on the road.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:57.490
So, naturally, interest for the model 3 is
going to be a lot higher.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:01.830
As a result, we can’t really compare model
3 interest with cybertruck interest until

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:05.259
the cybertruck has been on the road for a
few years as well.

00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:08.460
While this is a valid point, it doesn’t
really change much.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:12.479
From Tesla’s perspective, it makes a lot
more sense to double down on a vehicle that

00:06:12.479 --> 00:06:18.070
consumers are already convinced about as opposed
to trying to convince people about a new vehicle.

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:22.669
Something else to note is that while the Cybertruck
has millions of pre orders, we have no clue

00:06:22.669 --> 00:06:25.720
how many of these guys would actually follow
through.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:31.310
If the deposit amount was $1000 or $5000,
these pre orders would be a lot more meaningful,

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:35.539
but the deposit is only $100 and it’s fully
refundable.

00:06:35.539 --> 00:06:40.650
So, I feel that a lot of people just paid
the deposit to get in line, and they won’t

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:44.770
actually make a purchasing decision until
they actually have to pay the full amount.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:48.860
If we take a look at Tesla’s latest delivery
numbers, we’ll see that model s and x only

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:55.300
account for 14,700 deliveries while the model
3 and y account for 295,000 deliveries.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:07:00.080
And if the cybertruck is priced closer to
the s and x, it's likely that its delivery

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:04.600
numbers will be closer to that of the s and
x after the initial delivery hype dies down.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:08.930
Also, a lot of people who have cybertruck
pre orders also have pre orders with other

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:12.639
companies, and in the end, they’re only
going to go with 1.

00:07:12.639 --> 00:07:17.180
So, not only does Google Trends not paint
a pretty picture, but we have no gauge of

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:21.740
how good follow through will be, and these
factors just make it even less likely that

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:25.470
Tesla commits to production anytime soon.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:29.810
SUPPLY &amp; DEMAND:
While the demand for the Cybertruck isn’t

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:34.430
quite clear yet, the demand for Tesla’s
lower end vehicles is extremely high.

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:38.949
Even though Model 3 and Y prices have risen
substantially over the past several months,

00:07:38.949 --> 00:07:41.740
people still can’t get enough of these cars.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:46.050
Tesla is still selling every car they produce,
and they’re struggling to even keep up with

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:47.050
demand.

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:51.460
If I were to order a model 3 today, it would
take them 2 to 4 months to deliver it to me.

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:55.580
If I ordered a model y, it would take them
6 to 9 months to deliver it.

00:07:55.580 --> 00:08:00.599
And, if I order the model x, it would take
them 8 months to 1 year to deliver it.

00:08:00.599 --> 00:08:05.260
Also, I should mention that I live in Austin,
so you would think that deliveries are even

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:07.789
slower in other parts of the country.

00:08:07.789 --> 00:08:11.840
Considering this, a vehicle that has yet to
enter production is clearly not going to have

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:16.610
faster delivery estimates than vehicles that
have already been in production for years.

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:19.310
Something else to consider is the supply shortage.

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:23.400
Not only has the supply shortage been leading
to higher prices, but it’s also making it

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:25.990
even harder for Tesla to meet demand.

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:30.470
And the last thing you want to do is put the
limited resources that you do have into a

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:32.300
vehicle with high requirements.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:33.419
Take motors for example.

00:08:33.419 --> 00:08:37.760
Elon Musk has confirmed that initial Cybertruck
production will be quad motor cybertrucks.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:41.719
Meanwhile, the base level model 3 only has
1 motor.

00:08:41.719 --> 00:08:46.200
So, instead of building one quad motor cybertruck,
they could build 4 base level model 3s which

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:47.870
would be significantly more profitable.

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:51.790
The same argument could be made for battery
cells as well.

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:56.650
If resources were plentiful, it would be alright
to divert some resources into this behemoth,

00:08:56.650 --> 00:09:00.120
but when you’re strapped for resources,
it makes no sense to divert the resources

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:04.200
that you do have into a relatively low production
vehicle with high specifications.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:09.310
It’s simply not as profitable, and it sacrifices
the extra market share Tesla would have gained

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:11.940
by simply sticking to the model 3 and y.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:16.980
So, given the extraordinarily high demand
for the model 3 and y, it makes no sense to

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:21.519
focus on the cybertruck or any other vehicle
until Tesla can meet the demand for the 3

00:09:21.519 --> 00:09:22.640
and y first.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:28.370
And by the looks of it, that’s not happening
till at least the second half of this decade.

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:33.370
PR MOVE:
On top of supply chains, inflation, and lack

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:37.860
of follow through data pushing back the cybertruck,
we also have other promised releases pushing

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:39.240
back the cybertruck.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:43.500
The new Tesla roadster and the Tesla semi
were both announced years before the cybertruck

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:44.500
in 2017.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:49.200
Now, let’s say that supply chain concerns,
inflation, and demand for Tesla’s lower

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:51.560
end vehicles all cool off by 2025.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:55.070
At this point, which vehicle do you produce
first?

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:59.740
Sure, Cybertruck reservation holders would
have been waiting 6 years, Tesla roadster

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:03.030
reservation holders have been waiting for
8 years.

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:07.500
Not to mention, they had to pay $45,000 to
reserve their vehicle.

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:10.320
And what about Tesla Semi reservation holders?

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:14.470
These guys are institutional customers like
Walmart and Pepsi who paid millions to reserve

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:16.000
dozens of trucks.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:19.780
The last thing you wanna do is lose one of
these guys as a customer.

00:10:19.780 --> 00:10:24.920
Now, Tesla has started deliveries on the semi
truck, but I think you get the point.

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:29.020
Tesla has other promised products that may
take higher priority than the cybertruck which

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:31.980
will just push back production even more.

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:35.860
Considering all of these factors, the most
obvious conclusion that we come to is that

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:39.040
the Cybertruck was more or less just a PR
move.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:43.829
It was designed to generate a bunch of hype
for Tesla, and it did precisely that.

00:10:43.829 --> 00:10:48.240
The Cybertruck unveil literally kicked off
Tesla stock’s insane parabolic move which

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:51.889
has garnered the company more attention than
ever before.

00:10:51.889 --> 00:10:55.880
And this attention is likely leading to more
vehicle sales than ever before.

00:10:55.880 --> 00:11:00.940
So, whether the Cybertruck ever makes it to
the road or not, it was an extremely successful

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:01.940
product.

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:05.399
Now, this isn’t to say that the Cybertruck
will never make it to the road either.

00:11:05.399 --> 00:11:10.510
One of Elon’s pet peeves is concept cars
that never make it to the road, so I’m sure

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:14.399
Elon will eventually pump out the Cybertruck
at least out of principle.

00:11:14.399 --> 00:11:18.850
But, if you have a reservation and/or are
thinking about getting one, just know that

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:22.670
you’re likely going to be waiting at least
till 2024 and that’s if you spring for the

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:24.760
flagship quad motor version.

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:29.150
If you buy a lower end cybertruck, you’re
probably going to be waiting till 2026, but

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:31.170
that’s just what I think.

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:33.760
When do you guys think the Cybertruck will
hit the roads?

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:35.140
Comment that down below.

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:38.990
Also, drop a like if you agree that the cybertruck
was a brilliant marketing move.

